Chapter: E1000ResetLive
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Kernel chapter E1000e
  cites Kernel chapter Pci

 THE QUESTION THIS ANSWERS IS "WHAT DOES THE BED DO", AND THAT IS ALL IT
 ANSWERS. Sitting 10 banked `step=reset` and stopped: b3's bring-up hangs
 inside `e1000-reset`, while `nicinit` two stages earlier ran the identical
 seven operations and came back. The difference between them is the part's
 STATE -- `nicring` leaves the receiver ENABLED with a live ring
 (`rctl=67141658 rdt=15`) and b3 resets into that, where nicinit reset a part
 nothing had enabled.

 `E1000e.codex` already records that a bed run CANNOT speak to this: codex-vm
 does not model a DMA-in-flight reset hazard, so an arm that resets a live
 receiver there completes and proves nothing about the part. This chapter
 exists so that null is MEASURED and written down rather than assumed, because
 the failure it guards against is somebody later reading a green bed arm as
 evidence that resetting a live receiver is safe. It is not evidence. It is
 the bed agreeing with itself.

 THE LOOP EXPLANATIONS ARE ALREADY ELIMINATED AND NOT BY THIS ARM. Sitting 8's
 stepped bring-up banked `s2 await-reset ret 1 in 16us`, because CTRL is
 read-only on this part so the RST bit never sets and the loop's first read
 already sees it clear. `e1000-await-reset` therefore cannot be where five
 minutes goes, and `settle-mdio` is bounded by its own budget and was measured
 at 10036 us in the same flight. What is left inside `e1000-reset` is a single
 non-looping MMIO access that did not return, which is the part no longer
 answering -- and that is what sitting 11's split should land on.

 We say:

Section: Fixtures

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

  dev-of : PciScanResult -> Maybe PciDevice
  dev-of (scan) = e1000-find scan

Section: Entry

 The two arms differ in ONE thing: whether the receiver is running when RST is
 written. `quiesced` is what `e1000-quiesce` leaves behind and is the state the
 chapter's own convention says a caller owes; `live` is what `e1000-init`
 leaves behind and is what b3 reset into on the board.

  opening : [Console, Network.Read] Nothing = act
    let scan = pci-scan-bus 0
    in when dev-of scan
     is None -> print-line-uni "no part"
     is Just (d) ->
       let dev = e1000-init d
       in let mmio = dev.e-mmio
       in let rctl-live = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rctl
       in let live = e1000-reset mmio
       in let q = e1000-quiesce mmio
       in let rctl-quiet = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rctl
       in let quiet = e1000-reset mmio
       in act
         print-line-uni ("rctl while live    : " & show rctl-live)
         print-line-uni ("reset into live rx : " & show live)
         print-line-uni ("rctl after quiesce : " & show rctl-quiet)
         print-line-uni ("reset into quiet rx: " & show quiet)
         print-line-uni ("bed separates them : " & yn (live /= quiet))
       end
  end
